    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "It is clear that $10+a+b+ab=2(10+b)$.\nSimplifying, we get $(1-a)b=25$.\nSince $10, a, b, ab$ is an arithmetic progression, we have $a-b=b-ab$.\nSimplifying, we get $a=b(1-ab)$.\nSubstituting this into the previous equation, we get $(1-b^2)a^2=25$.\nThere are 3 cases to consider:\n1. $1-b^2=0 \\Rightarrow b=\\pm1$.\nIf $b=1$, then $(1-1)a^2=25 \\Rightarrow 0=25$, so no solutions.\nIf $b=-1$, then $(1-1)a^2=25 \\Rightarrow 0=25$.\n2. $1-b^2<0 \\Rightarrow b^2>1$.\nIf $b^2>1$, then $b\\neq-1$ and $a^2=25$.\nIf $b=-1$, we have no solutions.\nIf $b=1$, then $a^2<0$, so no solutions.\n3. $1-b^2>0 \\Rightarrow b^2<1$.\nIf $b^2<1$, then $b\\neq1$ and $a^2=b^2(1-b^2)$.\nIf $b=1$, then $a^2=1 \\Rightarrow a=\\pm1$, so two solutions.\nIf $b=\\frac{1}{2}$, then $a^2=\\frac{25}{4}$, so two solutions.\nIf $b=-\\frac{1}{2}$, then $a^2=\\frac{25}{4}$, so two solutions.\nIn total, we have 5 solutions.\nTherefore, the number of pairs $(a,b)$ of real numbers such that $10, a, b, ab$ is an arithmetic progression is $\\boxed{5}$.",
