      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "(1) Since $10, a, b, ab$, which is an arithmetic progression, we have $2a = 10 + ab$ and $2b = ab + 10$.\n\n(2) From $2a = 10 + ab$, we solve for $b$ to get $b = \\not{10 - 2a} \\implies b = \\boxed{10-2a}$.\n\n(3) Substituting $b$ into $2b = ab + 10$, we obtain $b = \\not{20 - a} \\implies b = \\boxed{20-a}$.\n\n(4) From $10 = a - ab + 10-2a - 20 + a$, we have $ab = -2$, which leads to $a = \\boxed{-\\frac{10}{b}}$.\n\n(5) Substituting $a = \\frac{-10}{b}$ into $ab =-2$, we obtain $b = \\boxed{-2}$. Finally, from $\\frac{-10}{-2} = \\frac{ab}{10}$, we have $b = \\not{1}$.\n\n(2004 AMC 12A Problem, Question \\#52, AIME 2002 Problem 11, Problem \\(‱\\))\n\nNote: The arithmetic progressions mentioned in the original problem are not arithmetic in the strictly regular sense but rather in a much more relaxed sense, so I have adapted the notations some.",
